    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i get the lock metaphor, but can u explain the rainbow table part more specifically? like how does the salt actually break the lookup process of a precomputed table?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Let me try to explain it in a way that's easy to grasp. Imagine you have a precomputed table of hashes, like a giant bookshelf with all the possible passwords and their corresponding hashes. Each entry in the bookshelf is like a treasure chest with a password and a corresponding hash.\n\nNow, when an attacker tries to crack a password, they look up the hash in the bookshelf to see if it matches any of the precomputed hashes. But, here's the thing: if the password is being hashed with a salt, it's like the attacker is looking for a specific book on the bookshelf with a unique title (the salt) and a specific password (the actual password).\n\nThe problem is, the attacker doesn't know the title of the book (the salt) that corresponds to the password they're trying to crack. So, even if they have a list of precomputed hashes, they can't just look up the hash and say, \"Ah, this must be the password!\" because they don't know the salt.\n\nThink of it like trying to find a specific book in a library. If you know the title of the book, you can easily find it. But if you don't know the title, you can",
